Ladybird keeps the CSP of a PDF’s response off the PDF viewer, which
brings its own. Gecko does the same: PdfStreamConverter, which hands
the PDF to its pdf.js viewer, clears the response’s
Content-Security-Policy and Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only
headers (“We trust PDF viewer, using no CSP”). Chromium shows a PDF in
the frame of its PDF extension, and PdfNavigationThrottle sets
should_ignore_initiator_policies_for_inheritance so the PDF’s frame
doesn’t inherit the navigation’s CSP. WebKit draws a PDF in its own
plugin (PDFPluginBase), with no page scripts for a CSP to stop.

Ladybird also drops the sandboxing flags that a PDF response’s CSP
adds, and keeps those of the viewer’s navigable. Gecko does the same,
since its CSP header is gone, while an iframe’s sandbox attribute
still reaches the viewer through its browsing context’s sandboxFlags
(which PdfStreamConverter’s supportsDownloading() checks). WebKit
loads a top-level PDF despite a CSP sandbox: SubframeLoader::
pluginIsLoadable() exempts a full main-frame PDF from the plugins flag
through shouldBypassCSPForPDFPlugin(). But it blocks a PDF in a
sandboxed frame, as Chromium does for any PDF navigation whose sandbox
flags to commit include the plugins flag (PdfNavigationThrottle).
